## Getting started

You need a JDK 17+ and sbt{% if full_stack %}, Node.js with npm{% endif %}{% if database != 'sqlite' %}, and Docker (for the development database and the database tests){% endif %}.
{%- if full_stack %}

The client uses [ScalablyTyped](https://github.com/ScalablyTyped/Converter) facades for React and MUI, built by the
standalone `stLib/` project with a locally published build of the converter (see `stLib/project/plugins.sbt`).
Publish them once, and again whenever `stLib/package.json` changes:

```bash
cd stLib && npm install && sbt --error publishLocal && cd ..
```

The first run takes several minutes and several GB of memory.
{%- endif %}

```bash
{%- if database != 'sqlite' and dev_compose %}
docker compose up -d                                  # the development database
{%- endif %}
{%- if full_stack %}
sbt client/webDebugDist                               # builds the client into debugDist/
STATIC_CONTENT_DIR=debugDist sbt server/run           # http://localhost:{{ http_port }}
{%- else %}
sbt server/run                                        # http://localhost:{{ http_port }}
{%- endif %}
```

Flyway creates the tables on startup. GraphiQL is at http://localhost:{{ http_port }}/api/graphiql.
{%- if database == 'sqlite' %}

The database is the file `{{ sqlite_file }}`. SQLite only supports a few forms of `ALTER TABLE`, so some schema
changes need a "create new table, copy, drop old" migration: see https://www.sqlite.org/lang_altertable.html.
{%- endif %}

## Tracing

The server traces GraphQL operations with OpenTelemetry. Set `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` (or `app.telemetry.endpoint`
in `application.conf`) to a collector's OTLP/HTTP endpoint, e.g. `http://localhost:4318`, and spans are exported there.
Without it, nothing is exported and tracing costs nothing.

{% if auth == 'zio-auth' %}## Logging in

zio-auth serves the whole login workflow: registration, login, password recovery and JWT sessions, over the users in
`UserStore` (the `app_user` table). {% if full_stack %}The client shows its login screens until someone is logged in (see `AuthGate`).{% endif %}

It is published to GitHub Packages, so building this project needs `GITHUB_TOKEN` set to a token with
`read:packages` (and `GITHUB_ACTOR`, if your user name differs from the one in `build.sbt`).

Two things to do before anyone but you uses it: set `AUTH_SECRET` (without it each restart signs sessions with a new
key, logging everyone out), and send the registration and password-recovery emails for real — `AuthModule.sendEmail`
logs them instead, so in development you follow the link from the server's log.

{% endif %}{% if ai == 'langchain4j' %}## AI

The `suggestDescription` mutation asks {{ ai_provider }} ({{ ai_model }}) for a one-line description. Configure it in
`app.ai` (`application.conf`){% if ai_provider != 'ollama' %}, and set `AI_API_KEY` in the environment{% else %}, with Ollama running locally{% endif %}. With
`provider = none`, AI calls report that AI isn't configured; nothing else changes.

{% endif %}## Testing

```bash
sbt test       # incremental: reruns only the tests whose code changed
sbt testFull   # everything
```

## Adding to it

`ModelObject` is a sample entity wired through every layer. To add your own, follow its pattern (every file involved
has `ModelObject` in its name), or ask Claude Code to "add a ... entity": the `add-entity` skill in `.claude/skills/`
walks through it.
{%- if packaging == 'docker' %}

## Packaging

`sbt server/Docker/publishLocal` builds a Docker image{% if full_stack %} that includes the production client build{% endif %}.
{%- elif packaging == 'systemd' %}

## Packaging

`sbt server/Debian/packageBin` builds a Debian package that installs the server as a systemd service{% if full_stack %},
including the production client build{% endif %}.
{%- endif %}

## Updating from the template

With a clean working tree, `copier update --trust --defaults` brings in the template's improvements. Resolve any
conflict markers, then run the tests.
